/* Subtypes in statically-typed OOP
   
Will Use made up programming language with:
- records with mutable fields half ML, half Java (like objects that have only
instance variables, no methods) -> get/set fields
- syntax mostly similar to ML (but with subtypes and mutable fields)
- type system with record types

Syntax
Record creation (like ML)
{f1=e1, f2=e2,..., fn=en}
evaluate ei, make record expression

Record field access (like OOP)
e.f
evaluate e to record v with an f field, get contents of f field

Record field update (like OOP)
e1.f = e2
evaluate e1 to record v1 and e2 to value v2, change v1's field (which must
exist) to v2, return v2

Record Types
{f1:t1, f2:t2,..., fn:tn} describes records that have field f1 holding type t1..

Type checking rules
- record creation: if e1 has type t1,..., en has type tn then record has type
{f1:t1,..., fn:tn}
- access record field: if e has record type containing field f of type t (f : t)
then e.f has type t. If record doesn't have field f, it doesn't type check
- record update: if e1 has record type containing field f : t and e2 has type t,
then e1.f = e2 has type t. After mutation f has same type then e.f has type t. 

Subtypes

Make type system more flexible
e.g. {x:real,y:real,c:string} is subtype of {x:real,y:real}, but
{x:real,c:string} isn't one since it misses y field

Expression {f1:t1, f2:t2,...,fn:tn} can also have type {f1:t1,fn:tn} with some
fields removed which is more generic. Functions which expect more generic type
with fields f1, f2 can be passed arguments with fields f1,f2 and f3 which
type-checks.

Changes to language to support subtypes:
1. add subtype relation: t1 <: t2 means t1 is subtype of t2. Binary relation
between 2 types, either one is subtype of the other or not (like 1 < 2)
Principle of subtitutability = every value of type t1 can be used in every way a
t2 can be
2. add typing rule that uses subtyping: if e has type t1 and t1<:t2 then e
(also) has type t2

Rules to meet substitutability test:
- "width" subtyping = a supertype can have a subset of fields with the same
types. A wider record can be subtype of slimmer record. We can drop fields to
get supertype.
{x:real,y:real, c:string} and {x:real,y:real}
- "permutation" subtyping =  a supertype can have the same set of fields with
the same types in a different order. We can reorder fields to get supertype.
{x:real,y:real} and {y:real,x:real}
- transitivity: if t1 <: t2 and t2 <: t3 then t1 <: t3
- reflexivity: every type is subtype of itself t <: t

Depth subtyping rule?
if ta <: tb, then {f1:t1,...,f:ta,..., fn:tn} <: {f1:t1,...,f:tb,..., fn:tn}?
We can go inside record type.
e.g. sphere of type {center: {x:real, y:real, z:real}, r:real} <: circle of type
{center: {x:real, y:real}, r:real} because depth subtyping (go into center
record) and width subtyping (supertype drops field z) {x:real, y:real, z:real}
<: {x:real, y:real}
This rule would break soundness so it shouldn't be added: because it allows
programs that can access missing record fields. It is not worth adding rule that
makes type system more flexible if it breaks soundness

e.g in pseudo-code (made up lanuage)
fun setToOrigin (c:{center:{x:real,y:real}, r:real}) = c.center = {x=0.0,y=
0.0} (*function that expects 2D circle, mutates center field*)
val sphere:{center:{x:real,y:real,z:real},r:real} = {center={x=3.0, y=4.0, z=0.
0}, r = 1.0}
val _ = setToOrigin(sphere)(* pass sphere as a subtype of circle would type
check. It mutates sphere so is not sphere any more, sphere has changed its type
from holding z field in center field to not holding it*)
val _ = sphere.center.z (*Would also type check since sphere's type has z,
but it would be runtime error since no z field any longer. Not sound since
didn't prevent missing field error.*)

If language with records/objects has getters and setters, depth subtyping
introduces unsoundness because subtyping shouldn't change the type of fields,
which would be possible with mutation.
If language with records/objects has getters, but no setters (fields are
immutable), depth subtyping is sound because subtyping just allows access to
field contents, doesn't mutate them.
A language must choose 2 out 3: setters, depth subtyping and soundness. Can't
be sound with setters and depth subtyping

Java/C# Arrays subtyping
Arrays should work just like records in terms of subtyping so Java/C# allow that
if t1 <: t2 then t1[] <: t2[] (an array of t1's is subtype of an array of t2's)
It allows array subtyping, but checks at run-time when we store into an array
that it's right type (not the supertype).
*/

