/*
Java/C# null
= keyword, not an object, hasn't fields or methods, like an empty record so it
could be a supertype of everything. Instead null is subtype of everything, can
have any object type. Wherever you must pass type t, you can pass also null, it
type checks, then get NullPointer exception when try to read field e.f or call
method e.m() if e is null. At run-time it checks if receiver is null and throws
NullPointer exception. It doesn't prevent this error statically.
It would be better to separate types that could be null (e.g. options in ML used
when you know the possibility you might not have any data) from types that
definitely are not null (strings, ints etc.). With ML's options you
are sure that those that are not options couln't be null, no need to check
at run-time to avoid exceptions.

Function subtyping
Caller can pass to a function an argument of subtype of the paramater the
function expects (because extra fields not used in body). Also result of
function call can be a subtype (ColorPoint) of expected result type (Point)
(because extra fields not used by caller).

A function type can be a subtype of another function type?
If a function expected parameter is itself a function of type t1->t2, can you
pass an argument of type t3->t4?

Return-type subtyping is OK
e.g.
(*computes distance moved from p to point resulted from applying f to p*)
fun distMoved (f:{x:real,y:real}->{x:real,y:real},p:{x:real,y:real}) =
    let val p2:{x:real,y:real} = f p (*apply f to p to get new point p2*)
        val dx:real = p2.x - p.x
	val dy:real = p2.y - p.y
    in Math.sqrt(dx*dx+dy*dy) end
(*flips point on both axis and adds color field*)
fun flipGreen p = {x=~p.x,y=~p.y,color="green"}
d = distMoved(flipGreen,{x=3.0,y=4.0})

Return type of flipGreen is ColorPoint, but distMoved expects as argument a
function with return type Point.
It's ok by adding rule: if ta <: tb, then function type t->ta <: t->tb.
A function can return more than it needs to, something with fields we don't need
(a subtype) = function types are covariant in their return type

Argument-type subtyping is wrong
e.g.
...
fun flipIfGreen p = if p.color = "green"
                    then {x=~p.x,y=~p.y}
                    else {x=p.x,y=p.y}
d = distMoved(flipIfGreen,{x=3.0,y=4.0})
Argument type of flipIfGreen is ColorPoint {x:real,y:real,color:string}, but it
is called with Point {x:real,y:real}.
We should make sure it doesn't type-check. It is unsound: ta <: tb does not
allow ta->t <: tb->t (flipIfGreen is not subtype of f). You can't assume this
function doesn't need all the fields, it might need color field so you can't
drop fields from arguments to functions.

Argument-type subtyping backwards is OK
e.g.
...
fun flipX_Y0 p = {x=~p.x,y=0.0}
d = distMoved(flipX_Y0,{x=3.0,y=4.0})
Argument type of flipX_Y0 is just {x:real}, but it is called with Point {x:real,
y:real}.
If tb <: ta then ta->t <: tb->t.
A function can assume less than it needs to about arguments. f needs flipX_Y0 to
work as thought it had type Point->Point and flipX_Y0 can act as if it had this
type even though it does not need its argument to have a y field.
= function types are covariant in their argument type (flipped)

Combine both
e.g.
...
fun flipXMakeGreen p = {x=~p.x,y=0.0,color="green"}
d = distMoved(flipXMakeGreen,{x=3.0,y=4.0})
flipXMakeGreen has type {x:real}->{x:real,y:real,color:string} which is subtype
of {x:real,y:real}->{x:real,y:real}.
flipXMakeGreen requires less than it's given as argument (only x field) and
returns more than it needs to (extra color field). So it can act as f.

If t3<:t1 and t2<:t4 then t1->t2 <: t3->t4
Function subtyping contravariant in argument(s) and covariant in results.
Instead of expected function f I can pass its subtype with as argument a
supertype of f's arg (needs less info) and return type subtype of f's return
(produce more info).
! Covariant arguments are wrong, contravariant arguments and covariant return
types. Arguments need to be flipped around to make subtypes otherwise it breaks
soundness of type system.

Subtyping for OOP (Java, C#)

Class names are also types: when declare class C you get also new type C.
Subclasses are also subtypes: if D subclass of C then D is subtype of C. Through
transitivity you're a subtype of anything higher than you in subclass hierarchy
all the way to Object at the top.
Substitution principle: instance of subclass could be used in place of instance
of superclass without raising issues with calling methods or access fields (like
instance variables in Ruby, but part of class definition so part of C's type)
that don't exist.
Object is essentially a record holding fields and methods:
- fields mutable (-> depth subtyping not sound)
- methods are immutable functions that also have access to self. Can be subtyped

Type system for subtypes/subclasses like for records types
- subtypes could have have extra fields and methods (like {x:real,y:real} for
{x:real}. Code will not care if the object has some extra fields that it
promised to have.
- overriding methods could have contravariant arguments and covariant results
compared to method overriden. The argument if subtype need's to be supertype of
what it was in the superclass while return type can be subtype of what it was in
superclass method. Sound because method slots are immutable.

If class A has a field of type t0 and a method m of type t1->t2 and subclass B
of class A that overrides m and take a t3 and returns a t4, for the type B to
be a subtype of the type A it needs that t1 <: t3 and t4 <: t2

In actual Java, C# a subclass can override a method with covariant return type
(considered overriding superclass's method if return type in subclass produces
more), but doesn't accept contravariant arguments (instead it makes a new method
in subclass with same name as the method in superclass, not-overriding it)

Class vs Type
- A class defines an object's behaviour. It has method definitions with bodies
with code and return things.
Subclassing inherits behavior and changes it via extension and overriding.
- A type describes an object's methods'arguments and result types (e.g.this type
has a method foo that takes a string and produces an int). But type is about
interface, doesn't include method bodies, just method signatures.
A subtype is substitutable in terms of its fields and method types.

In Java/C# a class name is both a class and a type. Both are sound:do not allow
subtypes to do things that would lead to "method missing" or accessing a field
at the wrong type.

Java fields in super/subclass
Superclass defines field foo of type t1, subclass can define field with same
name foo but different type t2. Now subclass has 2 fields named foo of types t1
and t2 in the same object. It depends of which class this object is an instance
of to know which field foo we are talking about with e.foo

self/this subclass OOP
Methods in objects might get an extra special argument self, which is not like
the other arguments contravariant, but covariant: self in method of subclass
(is an instance of the subclass, can have extra fields and methods) is subtype
of self in method of superclass (instance of superclass)

Generics vs subtyping OOP
Generics (type parameters, polymorphism) good when code can work for any type of
thing, but some of the things have to be the same type
- type for function that combine other functions
(* ('b->'c) * ('a->'b) -> ('a->'c)*)
fun compose (g,h) = fn x => g (h x)
- type for functions that operate on generic collection
val length : 'a list -> int
Subtyping not good for containers, would need to declare x and Y of type Object
so afterwards can set them as strings, int etc. (subtypes of Object), but when
retrievent data from pair would need to specify it's actualy not Object, but
string/int and would need to convert them at run type (downcasting).
*/
// generics in Java T1 and T2 works for any types T1 and T2
class Pair<T1,T2> {
    T1 x;
    T2 y;
    Pair(T1 _x, T2 _y){x = _x; y = _y;}
    Pair<T2,T1> swap(){
	return  new Pair<T2,T1>(y, x);
    }
}

/*
Subtypes are good when code needs object that behaves like a Foo, but fine to
use instead object that has more than Foo (subclass of Foo)
xe.g. ColorPoint instead of Point,
GUI widgets: superclass specifies big ideas anything that you put on the screen,
that responds to mouse clicks, can be resized, then things actually being passed
have much more (color, default font etc.)

Bounded polymorphism = use generics and subtypes together
Possible in Java, C#, C++
"Any type T1 that is a subtype of T2".
e.g. method that takes list of points and a circle (defined with center point
and radius) and returns new list of points from argument that lie within circle 
Would like to pass to method list of ColorPoint and get back list of ColorPoint.
Java/C# disallows since list of ColorPoint is not subtype of list of Point
(because depth subtyping unsound).

Given list of T's method inCircle will give back a list of T's, but it assumes
that T is subtype of Point. Caller needs to meet this constraint, must
instantiate T with a subtype of Point (including Point, e.g. ColorPoint), since
it needs to call its distance method)

pseudo-code:
List<T> inCircle(List<T> pts, Point center, double r) where T <: Point{
...
}
*/
//method inCircle assumes Point has distance method to other Point
List<Point> inCircle(List<Point> pts, Point center, double r){
    //create new empty collection
    List<Point> result = new ArrayList<Point>();
    //loop to check distance from all points to center and add to result
    for(Point pt: pts)
	if(pt.distance(center) <= r)
	    result.add(pt);
    return result;
}

// bounds
// use <> to introduce it's a generic method, specify that T is subtype of Point
<T extends Point> List<T> inCircle(List<T> pts, Point center, double r){
    List<T> result = new ArrayList<T>();
    for(T pt: pts)
	if(pt.distance(center) <= r)
	    result.add(pt);
    return result;
}

